  {
  "slug": "tds-quarterly-return-challan-deductee-reconciliation-guide",
  "category": "TDS & TCS",
  "title": "TDS Quarterly Return: Challan, Deductee and PAN Reconciliation Guide",
  "excerpt": "A step-by-step control process for matching TDS deductions, challans, deductee PANs and quarterly statements before filing.",
  "metaTitle": "TDS Quarterly Return Reconciliation Guide | FileFast",
  "metaDescription": "Learn how to reconcile TDS deductions, challans, PANs and deductee mismatches before quarterly TDS statement filing in India.",
  "content": "A quarterly TDS statement should agree with the deductor's books, payment records and challan information. A statement can be uploaded successfully and still contain errors that later appear as defaults, short deductions, unmatched challans or deductee-level mismatches. Reconciliation before filing reduces avoidable correction work.\\n\\n## 1. Build a quarter-wise deduction register\\nFor each payment, record the payee, PAN, payment or credit date, nature of payment, applicable section, gross amount, deduction rate, TDS amount and deposit date. The applicable trigger and rate depend on the provision and facts; do not apply an old template without checking current law.\\n\\n## 2. Verify deductee identity\\nCheck PAN spelling and structure against reliable records. Review cases where PAN is unavailable, invalid or inoperative and assess the applicable consequence under current rules. Keep employee and vendor records separate where reporting requirements differ.\\n\\n## 3. Match deductions to challans\\nFor every tax deposit, compare challan identification number, BSR code, deposit date, serial number, amount and allocation. A challan can be partly allocated across deductees, but the total allocation should reconcile to the available amount and statement period.\\n\\n## 4. Reconcile the ledger and payment evidence\\nCompare the TDS payable ledger with bank payment proof and challan status. Investigate late deposits, short payments, duplicate entries, reversals and amounts booked in a different quarter. Retain a note for every timing difference.\\n\\n## 5. Review validation output\\nUse the current authorised preparation utility or portal workflow. Review validation errors and warnings rather than repeatedly uploading an unchanged file. Confirm form type, quarter, financial year, deductor category and challan data before submission.\\n\\n## 6. Check post-filing processing\\nSave the provisional receipt or token number and check processing status. Review any default or intimation, identify the underlying reason and determine whether payment, correction or response is appropriate. A correction statement should be based on supporting evidence and the permitted process.\\n\\n## Common issues\\n- PAN quoted incorrectly or associated with the wrong deductee.\\n- Challan amount or allocation does not match the books.\\n- Payment is reported in the wrong quarter.\\n- Deduction date and deposit date are confused.\\n- Reversals or journal adjustments are omitted.\\n\\n## How FileFast can help\\nFileFast Global can assist with quarterly TDS data collation, challan reconciliation, statement preparation and eligible correction workflows. The final deduction position depends on transaction facts, applicable section, dates and current law. The deductor remains responsible for source records and timely deposit.\\n\\nOfficial reference: https://www.incometax.gov.in/"
}
