  {
  "slug": "udyam-msme-registration-delayed-payment-records-guide",
  "category": "MSME",
  "title": "Udyam Registration for MSMEs: Eligibility, Records and Delayed-Payment Readiness",
  "excerpt": "Understand Udyam registration, enterprise classification and records that help micro and small businesses monitor delayed receivables.",
  "metaTitle": "Udyam MSME Registration and Delayed Payment Guide",
  "metaDescription": "A practical guide to Udyam registration, MSME classification, business records and delayed-payment documentation for eligible enterprises.",
  "content": "Udyam Registration is the Government of India's online process for recognising eligible micro, small and medium enterprises. Registration details should match the enterprise's PAN, business constitution and linked information. Classification is determined under current investment and turnover criteria, not by choosing a preferred category.\\n\\n## 1. Confirm the enterprise profile\\nIdentify whether the applicant is a proprietorship, partnership, company, LLP or another eligible constitution. Keep PAN, GSTIN where applicable, business address, activity details and authorised contact information ready. Use the official Udyam portal and provide accurate information.\\n\\n## 2. Understand classification\\nMSME classification uses prescribed investment and turnover criteria. These thresholds can change through government notifications, so verify the current definition before advising a business or representing its category. Classification should not be inferred from a single bank balance or one month's sales.\\n\\n## 3. Prepare consistent information\\nCheck legal name, PAN, entity type, bank information, business activity and address. Ensure records across registrations are consistent or that legitimate differences can be explained. Keep the Udyam Registration Number and certificate safely after completion.\\n\\n## 4. Understand delayed-payment rules\\nThe MSME Development Act contains a framework for delayed payment to micro and small enterprises, subject to statutory definitions and conditions. A buyer's payment obligation and availability of a remedy depend on supplier status, transaction facts, acceptance or deemed acceptance and other legal requirements. Not every overdue invoice automatically qualifies.\\n\\n## 5. Keep receivable evidence\\nMaintain purchase orders, accepted delivery proof, invoices, e-invoices where applicable, correspondence, ledger statements, credit notes and payment history. Track invoice date, delivery or acceptance date, due date and outstanding amount. Reconcile receipts against invoices rather than relying only on net bank credits.\\n\\n## 6. Create a follow-up process\\nSend periodic statements, document disputes promptly and retain proof of communications. Escalate unresolved matters with a qualified adviser after reviewing the contract and statutory conditions.\\n\\n## How FileFast can help\\nFileFast Global can assist with Udyam application information, document organisation and basic business compliance coordination. Any delayed-payment claim, dispute strategy or legal proceeding requires a separate facts-based review and, where necessary, advice from a qualified legal professional.\\n\\nOfficial reference: https://udyamregistration.gov.in/"
}
